counterfactuals.output:
A method for arresting a runaway condition of a compression-ignition engine, the engine including an exhaust gas recirculation (EGR) actuator configured to regulate recirculation of exhaust gas to an intake manifold and an exhaust gas flow actuator configured to regulate exhaust gas discharge, may comprise:

detecting, by an electronic controller, an engine runaway condition based upon an engine speed exceeding a predetermined threshold, an engine acceleration exceeding a predetermined rate, or continued engine operation despite reduction or termination of commanded fuel delivery;

in response to detecting the engine runaway condition, commanding the EGR actuator toward an increased-flow position and commanding the exhaust gas flow actuator toward a flow-restricting position, thereby increasing exhaust manifold pressure relative to intake manifold pressure and inducing an increased flow of oxygen-depleted exhaust gas into the intake manifold;

coordinating respective positions of the EGR actuator and the exhaust gas flow actuator to reduce an oxygen concentration of an inducted charge below a concentration capable of sustaining uncontrolled combustion, while maintaining exhaust pressure and exhaust temperature within predetermined protection limits;

monitoring at least engine speed and, optionally, one or more of intake oxygen concentration, intake manifold pressure, exhaust manifold pressure, exhaust temperature, and EGR mass flow;

maintaining or progressively increasing the induced exhaust gas recirculation until engine speed decreases below a shutdown threshold or combustion ceases; and

thereafter releasing the exhaust gas flow restriction and returning the EGR actuator to a predetermined safe position.

The exhaust gas flow actuator may comprise an exhaust throttle, exhaust brake valve, variable-geometry turbine actuator, or other device capable of increasing exhaust backpressure. Coordination may be implemented using closed-loop control in which the controller determines actuator commands as a function of engine-speed error and one or more pressure, temperature, oxygen-concentration, or mass-flow constraints. The exhaust gas flow actuator is preferably not commanded to a fully closed position unless expressly permitted by a calibrated emergency mode, thereby limiting excessive exhaust pressure and mechanical loading.

In this manner, the EGR actuator supplies oxygen-depleted exhaust gas to the intake system while the exhaust gas flow actuator establishes the pressure differential necessary to drive such recirculation, thereby suppressing combustion even when the runaway condition is sustained by an uncontrolled fuel source independent of the commanded fuel-injection system.
